# Publish as xpengtoolkit, never personally

This repository is public and belongs to the `xpengtoolkit` account. Everything that
leaves this machine must carry that identity and nothing else.

## Before every commit

- Author and committer must be `xpengtoolkit <xpengtoolkit@users.noreply.github.com>`.
  The working copy sets this with `git config --local`, which overrides the global
  personal identity. Do not remove it, and do not commit with `--author` or
  `git -c user.name=...`.
- Check: `git log -1 --format='%an <%ae> %cn <%ce>'`

## Before every push, tag or release

- Check which identity will authenticate:
  `gh auth status` and
  `printf "protocol=https\nhost=github.com\n\n" | git credential fill | grep '^username='`
- Both must say `xpengtoolkit`. If either says anything else, STOP and ask. Do not
  push, tag or create a release with a personal account or token, not even once.
- A GitHub release is publicly attributed to whoever created it, so this applies to
  `gh release create` as much as to `git push`.

## Never publish

- Personal email addresses or names, machine paths (`/Users/...`), credentials for
  the maintainer's own hosts (the HA URL, SSH passwords), or unmasked VINs (see
  `mask_vin` in `vin_descriptor.py`).
- Certificate or key material, `xpeng_tokens.json`, `xpeng_tenant.json`. `.gitignore`
  covers these; confirm with `git diff --cached --name-only` before committing.

## Why this is written down

Measured 2026-10-13: the working copy committed as `xpengtoolkit` correctly, but `gh`
and the macOS keychain both authenticated as the maintainer's personal account, so a
plain `git push` - and any release created with `gh` - would have been published as
that account. The commits were fine; the authorisation was not.
